---
import Prose from '../layouts/Prose.astro';
import BenchChart from '../components/BenchChart.astro';
import bench from '../data/bench.json';
import { url } from '../lib/url.ts';
import { accuracy, basicPerQuestion, change, datasets, primerTokens, questionCount, sparse, type Fmt, type PerQuestion } from '../lib/results.ts';

const fmt = (n: number) => n.toLocaleString('en-US');
const pct = (n: number, base: number) => {
  const d = Math.round((n / base - 1) * 100);
  return d === 0 ? '±0%' : d > 0 ? `+${d}%` : `−${-d}%`;
};
const formats: Fmt[] = ['json', 'toon', 'lwon'];
const sparseTotal = sparse.accuracy.reduce((n, a) => n + a.cells.lwon.total, 0);
const sparseMissed = Object.fromEntries(formats.map((f) => [f, sparse.accuracy.reduce((n, a) => n + a.cells[f].total - a.cells[f].correct, 0)])) as Record<Fmt, number>;
const headings = [
  { depth: 2, slug: 'tokens', text: 'Claude tokens' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'accuracy', text: 'Accuracy' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'sparse', text: 'Harder test: sparse data' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'cost', text: 'Cost per question' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'o200k', text: 'Another tokenizer' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'method', text: 'Method' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'limits', text: 'Limits' },
  { depth: 2, slug: 'reproduce', text: 'Reproduce' },
];
---
<Prose title="Benchmarks" description="Claude token counts and answer accuracy for JSON, TOON and LWON on six realistic datasets, with method and limits." headings={headings}>
  <h1>Benchmarks</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    How many tokens the same data costs as JSON, TOON and LWON, and whether Claude still answers
    questions about it correctly. Every LWON result is decoded again and checked against the original.
  </p>
  <div class="wide"><BenchChart /></div>

  <h2 id="tokens">Claude tokens</h2>
  <p>
    Counted with the Claude <code>count_tokens</code> API; Haiku, Sonnet and Opus 5.5 share a tokenizer.
    The last column adds the LWON primer, about {primerTokens} tokens, which a model needs once per conversation.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="table">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">Claude tokens per format and dataset</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Dataset</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">JSON minified</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">TOON</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">LWON</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">LWON vs JSON</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">LWON + primer</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {datasets.map((d) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><span class="ds">{d.name}</span><span class="muted">{d.shape}</span></th>
            <td class="num">{fmt(d.claude.json)}</td>
            <td class="num">{fmt(d.claude.toon)}</td>
            <td class="num"><strong>{fmt(d.claude.lwon)}</strong></td>
            <td class="num">{pct(d.claude.lwon, d.claude.json)}</td>
            <td class="num">{fmt(d.lwonWithPrimer)} <span class="muted">({pct(d.lwonWithPrimer, d.claude.json)})</span></td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    LWON is smaller than minified JSON on every dataset and smaller than TOON on all but the search
    result, where long free-text snippets dominate and TOON is 4% smaller. On the 20-node menu the
    primer costs more than LWON saves, so small payloads are better sent as JSON.
  </p>

  <h2 id="accuracy">Accuracy</h2>
  <p>
    Each model answered {questionCount} questions per format about the same datasets: finding a record,
    following nested fields, counting and summing, walking the tree, and telling an absent key from a
    null one. Answers were checked against values computed from the data.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="table">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">Correct answers per model and format</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Model</th>
          {formats.map((f) => <th scope="col" class="num">{f.toUpperCase()}</th>)}
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {accuracy.map((a) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{a.label}</th>
            {formats.map((f) => (
              <td class="num" class:list={['num', { miss: a.cells[f].correct < a.cells[f].total }]}>
                {a.cells[f].correct}/{a.cells[f].total}
              </td>
            ))}
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Every model answered every question correctly in every format, including Haiku with thinking turned
    down to low effort. LWON 0.x, which wrote every key by position, missed one count at low effort; the
    current encoding did not.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sparse">Harder test: sparse data</h2>
  <p>
    150 log events where most fields are optional and some are null: {fmt(sparse.tokens.json.raw)} Claude
    tokens as JSON, {fmt(sparse.tokens.toon.raw)} as TOON and {fmt(sparse.tokens.lwon.raw)} as LWON
    ({change(sparse.tokens.lwon.raw, sparse.tokens.json.raw)}). The questions count keys across rows,
    add up values, list the keys of one row, and ask whether a key is absent or null. Each was asked
    {sparse.trials} times to Haiku and 2 times to Sonnet.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="table">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">Correct answers on the sparse data per model and format</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Model</th>{formats.map((f) => <th scope="col" class="num">{f.toUpperCase()}</th>)}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {sparse.accuracy.map((a) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{a.label}</th>
            {formats.map((f) => <td class="num">{a.cells[f].correct}/{a.cells[f].total}</td>)}
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Over all runs, models got {sparseMissed.lwon} of {sparseTotal} answers wrong with LWON,
    {sparseMissed.json} with JSON and {sparseMissed.toon} with TOON. Every wrong answer, in every
    format, was a count over 150 rows that was off by one to three, or a sum; every question about a
    single row, including absent versus null, was answered correctly. With this many questions, LWON and
    JSON are within noise of each other. TOON counted best: on irregular data it writes one
    <code>key: value</code> line per field and states the row count, which makes keys easy to tally,
    at {change(sparse.tokens.toon.raw, sparse.tokens.lwon.raw)} more tokens than LWON.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cost">Cost per question</h2>
  <p>
    Fewer input tokens is only half the bill. Models also spend output tokens, including thinking, to
    read the data, and output costs several times more than input. <em>Sent once</em> prices the data as
    normal input; <em>cached</em> prices it as a cache read, when the same data answers many questions.
  </p>
  <p>
    LWON 0.x wrote every key by position, so on sparse data models had to count positions through rows
    full of <code>~</code>: Haiku thought about four times as long as with JSON. LWON now writes keys
    that most rows lack by name, in a <code>*</code> slot, which removed most of that extra thinking.
    The tables compare both.
  </p>
  {([['Regular data (six datasets)', basicPerQuestion], ['Sparse data', sparse.perQuestion]] as [string, PerQuestion[]][]).map(([title, rows]) => (
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="table">
        <caption>{title}: change in cost against JSON</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Model</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">Output tokens JSON / LWON 0.x / LWON</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">LWON sent once</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">LWON cached</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">LWON 0.x cached</th>
            <th scope="col" class="num">TOON sent once</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{r.label}</th>
              <td class="num">{fmt(Math.round(r.cells.json.output))} / {r.cells['lwon-positional'] ? fmt(Math.round(r.cells['lwon-positional'].output)) : '–'} / {fmt(Math.round(r.cells.lwon.output))}</td>
              <td class="num" class:list={['num', r.cells.lwon.once < r.cells.json.once ? 'good' : 'miss']}>{change(r.cells.lwon.once, r.cells.json.once)}</td>
              <td class="num" class:list={['num', r.cells.lwon.cached < r.cells.json.cached ? 'good' : 'miss']}>{change(r.cells.lwon.cached, r.cells.json.cached)}</td>
              <td class="num">{r.cells['lwon-positional'] ? change(r.cells['lwon-positional'].cached, r.cells.json.cached) : '–'}</td>
              <td class="num">{change(r.cells.toon.once, r.cells.json.once)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  ))}
  <p>
    On regular data LWON is now the cheapest of the three formats for every model when the data is sent
    once, and cheaper than JSON when it is cached and reused. On sparse data with counting it is cheaper
    than JSON sent once and slightly more expensive cached, where LWON 0.x cost up to three times as much.
  </p>

  <h2 id="o200k">Another tokenizer</h2>
  <p>The same data counted with {bench.tokenizer}, used by other model families.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="table">
      <caption class="visually-hidden">o200k tokens per format and dataset</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Dataset</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">JSON indented</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">JSON minified</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">TOON</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">LWON</th>
          <th scope="col" class="num">LWON vs JSON</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {datasets.map((d) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row"><span class="ds">{d.name}</span></th>
            <td class="num">{fmt(d.o200k.pretty)}</td>
            <td class="num">{fmt(d.o200k.json)}</td>
            <td class="num">{fmt(d.o200k.toon)}</td>
            <td class="num"><strong>{fmt(d.o200k.lwon)}</strong></td>
            <td class="num">{pct(d.o200k.lwon, d.o200k.json)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="method">Method</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Datasets are generated from a fixed seed so every run is identical. You can load each one in the <a href={url('/playground')}>playground</a>.</li>
    <li>The baseline is minified JSON. Indented JSON is shown only for reference; comparing against it would overstate the savings.</li>
    <li>TOON output uses {bench.toon} with default options; LWON uses the reference encoder with default options.</li>
    <li>In the accuracy test, JSON and TOON get a one-line label and LWON gets its primer. Each question is asked once per model and format, with adaptive thinking at medium effort unless noted.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The datasets are synthetic, shaped after common API and tool payloads, and up to about 8,000 tokens.</li>
    <li>The regular-data test sits at the ceiling: it shows LWON does not hurt accuracy there, not small differences. The sparse test separates the formats, but with 22 questions and 2–3 trials, a difference of a few answers is within noise.</li>
    <li>LWON gets a primer of about {primerTokens} tokens; JSON and TOON get a one-line label.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="reproduce">Reproduce</h2>
  <pre class="code"><code>git clone https://github.com/ahmetzencirli/lwon
cd lwon
npm install
npm run bench                 # o200k token counts
npm run eval:tokens           # Claude token counts (needs ANTHROPIC_API_KEY)
npm run eval -- --budget 2    # accuracy test, about $1.30 for three models
npm run eval -- --suite sparse --trials 3 --budget 2
npm run eval:report
npm run eval:report -- --suite sparse</code></pre>
</Prose>

<style>
  .wide { margin: 28px 0 8px; }
  .ds { display: block; font-weight: 600; }
  th[scope='row'] .muted { font-weight: 400; font-size: 0.82rem; }
  .table th[scope='row'] { background: none; font-size: 0.92rem; min-width: 10em; }
  .table-wrap :global(table) { border: 0; display: table; }
  td.miss { color: var(--danger); font-weight: 600; }
  td.good { color: var(--ok); font-weight: 600; }
  caption:not(.visually-hidden) { caption-side: top; text-align: left; padding: 12px 14px 4px; font-weight: 600; font-size: 0.9rem; }
</style>
